WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.029 --> 00:00:01.570
EMILY YE: Developers
often need a way

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:02.980
to generate
temporary credentials

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:04.390
to access their Cloud accounts.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:05.765
In this video,
we'll show you how

00:00:05.765 --> 00:00:07.960
to generate temporary
Google Cloud Platform IAM

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.910
credentials as needed
using HashiCorp Vault.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:18.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.790
Hi, I'm Emily, an engineer
working on Google Cloud

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.370
Platform, an open source
integration between Cloud

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:24.204
and HashiCorp Vault.

00:00:24.204 --> 00:00:26.078
ANDY MANOSKE: And I'm
Andy, a product manager

00:00:26.078 --> 00:00:27.710
at HashiCorp on the Vault team.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:29.190
Vault is a secrets
management tool

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.530
that allows you to store,
manage, and control access

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.240
to tokens, passwords,
certificates,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.467
and other types of secrets.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:37.050
EMILY YE: In our last video, we
used HashiCorp Vault to secure

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.680
an API key for our '80s-style
arcade game, "Hyperspace,"

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.590
and gave our GCE instances
an easy way to access secrets

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.860
on GCP using the Vault. Now
let's talk about other ways we

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.660
can use Vault on GCP.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.230
Let's say we want to add a daily
leaderboard to our video game.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.090
We'll have it update
at midnight each day,

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.220
so it needs to access our
video game data stored

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.247
in Spanner at least once a day.

00:00:58.247 --> 00:01:00.330
Should we just give it its
own service account key

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.789
and access to our
Spanner database?

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:04.371
ANDY MANOSKE: Well, what happens
if someone steals the service

00:01:04.371 --> 00:01:05.246
account key?

00:01:05.246 --> 00:01:07.620
If you don't have it locked
down as well as the main app,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.320
and it's all on
automated process,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.160
how would you detect
if it gets stolen?

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.182
That won't be good.

00:01:12.182 --> 00:01:13.890
What if you just gave
it temporary access

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.470
to your project, like a
temporary IAM credential?

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.845
EMILY YE: Yeah, that would work.

00:01:17.845 --> 00:01:20.345
ANDY MANOSKE: So check out the
GCP secrets backend for Vault

00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:20.845
then.

00:01:20.845 --> 00:01:22.860
A secret backend
in HashiCorp Vault

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.950
is a pluggable interface that
can store, generate, or alter

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:26.790
secrets.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.740
The GCP secrets
backend dynamically

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.310
creates OAuth access tokens
based on IAM service accounts

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.550
and policies.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.430
This means you can
generate credentials

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.830
to access GCP as
needed, without needing

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.900
to worry about how long
those credentials last.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.080
EMILY YE: That actually sounds
like exactly what I need.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.480
I could see how this would be
useful in other cases, too.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.670
Things like batch jobs or
virtual training accounts

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.250
don't need access to your
GCP project for very long

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.110
but can be hard
to clean up after.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.300
Developers could use
Vault's GCP secrets

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.040
backend to assign the
minimum permissions

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.450
to their initial
service accounts

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.815
and obtain additional
permissions only as needed.

00:01:59.815 --> 00:02:01.190
ANDY MANOSKE: Want
to try it out?

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:02.400
EMILY YE: Sure.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.650
So as you can see, we already
have our Vault server up

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.812
and running on GCP
from last time.

00:02:06.812 --> 00:02:08.520
We'll show you how a
server admin sets up

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.910
the GCP secrets backend and
how a user obtains a new secret

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.714
or a GCP OAuth access token.

00:02:13.714 --> 00:02:16.130
ANDY MANOSKE: The admin needs
to add configuration details

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.460
to his credentials.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.020
The Vault server
uses this config

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.944
to make requests to GCP
to set up IAM permissions.

00:02:21.944 --> 00:02:24.360
EMILY YE: Next, the admin needs
to add Vault-specific role

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:24.850
sets.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.270
These determine what permissions
the service accounts created

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.061
by Vault will have.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.190
The bindings argument
is a configuration file

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.945
where we specify GCP IAM roles
to assign to each resource.

00:02:36.945 --> 00:02:38.570
ANDY MANOSKE: Can we
take a closer look

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:39.830
at this bindings config file?

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.455
EMILY YE: Sure.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.940
Each block in the
configuration file

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.462
specifies a resource
and a set of IAM roles.

00:02:48.462 --> 00:02:49.920
As you can see in
this block, we've

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.920
given the resource name
for our Spanner database

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.990
that contains our scoring
data and the role of database

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:55.820
reader.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.960
This tells Vault to give
generated service accounts

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.502
permissions to read the scoring
data from the given database.

00:03:00.502 --> 00:03:02.959
ANDY MANOSKE: So the secrets
backend is set up then, right?

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:03.601
EMILY YE: Yep.

00:03:03.601 --> 00:03:05.100
Let's switch to a
different terminal

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.742
now and show how our leaderboard
app gets an access token.

00:03:09.742 --> 00:03:11.700
ANDY MANOSKE: To obtain
our OAuth access token,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.590
we read from the creds
endpoint, specifying

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.791
the rule set we want to
generate the token under.

00:03:15.791 --> 00:03:18.290
Vault generates the token using
a service account it creates

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:20.662
and then returns this token.

00:03:20.662 --> 00:03:22.620
EMILY YE: When the TTL,
or Time To Live, is up,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.828
Vault revokes the lease and
the access token in turn.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.110
We can then use this OAuth
token to call the Spanner API.

00:03:33.892 --> 00:03:35.850
Now that we've finished
our request of Spanner,

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:36.990
we don't need access.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:38.400
Let's revoke this
token manually.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.330
That was really easy to do.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.010
Andy, if other
developers have questions

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.450
on setting this
backend up, where

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:49.877
can they get more information?

00:03:49.877 --> 00:03:51.960
ANDY MANOSKE: So the
vaultproject.io page and look

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.240
up documentation on the
GCP secrets backend.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.200
EMILY YE: Thanks, Andy.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.866
You can also check
out other information

00:03:56.866 --> 00:03:58.380
for running Vault
on GCP, including

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.810
using the GCP
authentication backends

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.660
or using GCP with
other HashiCorp tools,

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:04.350
like Terraform.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.550
See the links in
description below.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:07.050
Bye.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.562
ANDY MANOSKE: Bye.

00:04:08.562 --> 00:04:10.270
EMILY YE: Thanks,
everyone, for watching.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.140
We hope you found this series on
using HashiCorp Vault with GCP

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.010
helpful.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.600
Check out the links
in the description

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.920
below, and don't forget to
subscribe to the channel.

